---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
---

<DocsLayout title="CLI (appmctl)" activeNav="/guides/cli">
  <h1>CLI — <code>appmctl</code></h1>
  <p>
    <code>appmctl</code> is a dependency-light command-line client for the App Manager API.
    It is distributed as a single Bash 3.2-compatible executable, making it suitable for
    CI runners, minimal build images, and local automation without a language-specific SDK.
  </p>
  <p>
    The CLI is currently pre-1.0 (<code>0.2.0</code>). Pin the source commit in reproducible
    pipelines and expect the command surface to evolve alongside API v1.
  </p>

  <h2>Requirements</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Tool</th><th>Required for</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Bash 3.2+</td><td>All commands</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>curl</code></td><td>API requests</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>jq</code></td><td><code>--json</code> output, <code>stream</code>, and formatted subcommand output</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>mktemp</code>, <code>tee</code>, <code>dd</code></td><td><code>stream</code> and secure temporary files</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Install</h2>

  <h3>curl + bash (recommended for CI)</h3>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`curl -fsSL \\
  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/karerckor/app-manager/main/libs/cli/install.sh | bash

export PATH="$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH"
appmctl --version`} />

  <p>Install to a custom directory:</p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`curl -fsSL \\
  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/karerckor/app-manager/main/libs/cli/install.sh | \\
  APPMCTL_INSTALL_DIR="$PWD/.appmctl-bin" bash

export PATH="$PWD/.appmctl-bin:$PATH"`} />

  <h3>Pin to a specific commit</h3>
  <p>
    The installer downloads from <code>main</code> unless <code>APPMCTL_REF</code> is set, which is why
    production CI should always pin a full commit SHA and verify the checksum.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`APPMCTL_REF='FULL_GIT_COMMIT_SHA'

curl -fsSL \\
  "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/karerckor/app-manager/$&#123;APPMCTL_REF&#125;/libs/cli/install.sh" | \\
  APPMCTL_REF="$APPMCTL_REF" \\
  APPMCTL_SHA256="expected-sha256-hash" \\
  bash`} />

  <h3>From a local checkout</h3>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`libs/cli/bin/appmctl --help`} />

  <h2>Configuration</h2>
  <p>The API key and team slug are required. Set them via environment variables:</p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`export APP_MANAGER_API_KEY='1|plain-text-api-key'
export APP_MANAGER_TEAM='mobile-team'
export APP_MANAGER_APP='customer-app'            # optional default app
export APP_MANAGER_INSTANCE_URL='https://manager.example.com'  # optional`} />

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Variable</th><th>CLI override</th><th>Required</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>APP_MANAGER_API_KEY</code></td><td>—</td><td>Yes</td><td>Bearer API key</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>APP_MANAGER_TEAM</code></td><td><code>--team</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Team slug</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>APP_MANAGER_INSTANCE_URL</code></td><td><code>--instance-url</code></td><td>No</td><td>Server URL (default: <code>http://localhost:8000</code>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>APP_MANAGER_APP</code></td><td><code>--app</code></td><td>No</td><td>Default app slug for app-scoped commands</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>APP_MANAGER_POLLING_INTERVAL</code></td><td>—</td><td>No</td><td>Poll interval in seconds for <code>builds wait</code>, <code>builds when-running</code>, and <code>stream --track</code> (default: <code>10</code>)</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The API key is accepted <strong>only</strong> through the environment variable, never as a CLI argument.
    The CLI never prints the API key in dry-run output.
  </p>

  <h2>Global options</h2>
  <p>Global options go before the resource name: <code>appmctl --json builds list</code>.</p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Option</th><th>Description</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>--json</code></td><td>Pretty-print and validate a JSON response with <code>jq</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--dry-run</code></td><td>Print the request plan without calling the API</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>-o, --output FILE</code></td><td>Save the response instead of writing it to stdout</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--force</code></td><td>Allow <code>--output</code> to replace an existing file</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--team TEAM</code></td><td>Override <code>APP_MANAGER_TEAM</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--instance-url URL</code></td><td>Override <code>APP_MANAGER_INSTANCE_URL</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--app APP</code></td><td>Override <code>APP_MANAGER_APP</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>-h, --help</code></td><td>Show help</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--version</code></td><td>Show the CLI version</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Most options are also accepted after the action, so <code>appmctl builds list --json</code> works too.
    The parent directory of <code>--output</code> must already exist, and existing files are protected
    unless <code>--force</code> is explicit.
  </p>

  <h2>Commands</h2>
  <p>
    All subcommands support <code>--help</code> for detailed usage and
    <code>--dry-run</code> to preview the request without sending it.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Resource</th><th>Actions</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>builds</code></td><td><code>list</code>, <code>get</code>, <code>create</code>, <code>cancel</code>, <code>patch</code>, <code>wait</code>, <code>when-running</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>logs</code></td><td><code>get</code>, <code>post</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>artifacts</code></td><td><code>list</code>, <code>get</code>, <code>upload</code>, <code>delete</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>files</code></td><td><code>list</code>, <code>show</code>, <code>get</code>, <code>upload</code>, <code>update</code>, <code>delete</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>assets</code></td><td><code>list</code>, <code>show</code>, <code>download</code>, <code>upload</code>, <code>delete</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>apps</code></td><td><code>list</code>, <code>get</code>, <code>create</code>, <code>update</code>, <code>delete</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>credentials</code></td><td><code>list</code>, <code>get</code>, <code>create</code>, <code>update</code>, <code>delete</code>, <code>download</code>, <code>upload</code>, <code>types</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>integrations</code></td><td><code>list</code>, <code>get</code>, <code>repos</code>, <code>workflows</code>, <code>refs</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>pipelines</code></td><td><code>list</code>, <code>get</code>, <code>create</code>, <code>update</code>, <code>delete</code>, <code>run</code>, <code>inputs</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>webhooks</code></td><td><code>list</code>, <code>get</code>, <code>create</code>, <code>update</code>, <code>delete</code>, <code>test</code>, <code>rotate</code>, <code>deliveries</code>, <code>redeliver</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>request</code></td><td>Generic access to any team endpoint (legacy)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>stream</code></td><td>Run a command and upload its output to a build log (legacy)</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3><code>appmctl builds</code></h3>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl builds list [--app APP] [--status STATUS] [--page N]
appmctl builds get [--app APP] BUILD_ID
appmctl builds create [--app APP] --profile TYPE [options]
appmctl builds cancel [--app APP] BUILD_ID
appmctl builds patch [--app APP] BUILD_ID [--status STATUS] [--tag NAME[:#HEX]]
appmctl builds wait [--app APP] BUILD_ID [--timeout SECONDS]
appmctl builds when-running [--app APP] BUILD_ID [--timeout SECONDS] -- COMMAND [ARG ...]`} />

  <p><code>create</code> options:</p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Option</th><th>Description</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>--profile TYPE</code></td><td>Build profile, e.g. <code>production</code>, <code>staging</code>, <code>appstore</code> (required)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--request-uuid UUID</code></td><td>Idempotency key for CI retries; generated automatically when omitted</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--context JSON</code></td><td>Inline JSON context/metadata</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--reporting-mode MODE</code></td><td><code>manual</code> (default) or <code>eas</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--expo-build-id ID</code></td><td>Expo/EAS build ID; required when <code>--reporting-mode eas</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--app-identifier-id UUID</code></td><td>Target app identifier</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--tag NAME[:#HEX]</code></td><td>Attach a tag; repeatable</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`# List builds for an app
appmctl builds list --app customer-app --status running

# Show build details
appmctl builds get bld-abc123

# Create a manual build
appmctl builds create --app customer-app --profile production

# Create a build with an idempotency key and colored tags
appmctl builds create --profile production \\
  --request-uuid "$(uuidgen)" \\
  --context '{"runner":"github"}' \\
  --tag ios:#3b82f6 --tag 1.0.0:#f59e0b

# Register an EAS build
appmctl builds create --profile production \\
  --reporting-mode eas --expo-build-id abc-123

# Cancel a build
appmctl builds cancel bld-abc123`} />

  <p>
    <code>patch</code> updates a build's status, its tags, or both — at least one of
    <code>--status</code> or <code>--tag</code> is required. Status transitions apply to manually
    reported builds only; tags can be set on any build. <code>--tag</code> replaces the whole tag set.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`# Report a terminal status (manual builds)
appmctl builds patch bld-abc123 --status completed

# Replace the tag set (any build)
appmctl builds patch bld-abc123 --tag ios:#3b82f6 --tag 1.0.0`} />

  <h4>Waiting on builds</h4>
  <p>
    <code>wait</code> polls until the build reaches a terminal state and exits with a status that
    reflects the outcome, so a CI job can gate on it directly. Polling happens every
    <code>APP_MANAGER_POLLING_INTERVAL</code> seconds.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`# Block until the build finishes, at most one hour
appmctl builds wait bld-abc123 --timeout 3600`} />
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Exit code</th><th><code>builds wait</code></th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>0</code></td><td>Build completed successfully</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>1</code></td><td>Build failed</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>130</code></td><td>Build was cancelled</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>124</code></td><td>Timeout exceeded (only when <code>--timeout</code> is given; otherwise it waits indefinitely)</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    <code>when-running</code> checks the build once and runs the command only if the build is
    currently <code>pending</code>, <code>running</code>, or <code>cancel_requested</code>. While the
    command runs it keeps polling and terminates the command if the build reaches a terminal state.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`# Skip the step entirely if the build is no longer active
appmctl builds when-running bld-abc123 -- make deploy`} />
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Exit code</th><th><code>builds when-running</code></th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>command's own status</td><td>The command ran to completion — its exit status is passed through</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>130</code></td><td>The build was cancelled while the command was running; the command was terminated</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>1</code></td><td>The build was already terminal, or its status could not be read</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    <code>when-running</code> accepts <code>--timeout</code> but currently ignores it: it does not
    wait for a pending build to become active, so the flag has no effect on behavior. Use
    <code>builds wait</code> when you need a bounded wait.
  </p>

  <h3><code>appmctl logs</code></h3>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl logs get [--app APP] --build-id ID
appmctl logs post [--app APP] --build-id ID --content TEXT`} />
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`# Fetch build logs
appmctl logs get --build-id bld-abc123

# Append log content
appmctl logs post --build-id bld-abc123 --content "compile: finished\\n"`} />
  <p>
    For long-running commands prefer <code>appmctl stream</code>, which uploads output while the
    command runs instead of posting it after the fact.
  </p>

  <h3><code>appmctl artifacts</code></h3>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl artifacts list [--app APP] --build-id ID
appmctl artifacts get [--app APP] --build-id ID ARTIFACT_ID [-o FILE]
appmctl artifacts upload [--app APP] --build-id ID --name NAME [--metadata JSON] --file FILE
appmctl artifacts delete [--app APP] --build-id ID ARTIFACT_ID`} />
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`# List artifacts for a build
appmctl artifacts list --build-id bld-abc123

# Download an artifact
appmctl artifacts get --build-id bld-abc123 art-001 -o app.aab

# Upload an artifact
appmctl artifacts upload --build-id bld-abc123 \\
  --name app-release.aab \\
  --metadata '{"channel":"internal"}' \\
  --file build/app-release.aab

# Delete an artifact
appmctl artifacts delete --build-id bld-abc123 art-001`} />

  <h3><code>appmctl files</code></h3>
  <p>
    Files are the app's general-purpose media library: mapping files, provisioning notes,
    release checklists, and anything else attached to an app rather than to a single build.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl files list [--app APP] [--per-page N]
appmctl files show [--app APP] --name NAME
appmctl files get [--app APP] --name NAME [-o FILE]
appmctl files upload [--app APP] --file FILE [--metadata JSON]
appmctl files update [--app APP] --name NAME --metadata JSON
appmctl files delete [--app APP] --name NAME`} />
  <p>
    Files are addressed by name, not by id. <code>show</code> returns metadata,
    <code>get</code> downloads the content. Names are percent-encoded for you but must not
    contain slashes — use <code>appmctl request</code> for names outside the ASCII range.
    <code>--metadata</code> takes a JSON object, or the literal <code>null</code> to clear it.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`# List files attached to an app
appmctl files list --app customer-app

# Attach a file with metadata
appmctl files upload --file ./mapping.txt --metadata '{"channel":"beta"}'

# Inspect metadata without downloading the content
appmctl files show --name mapping.txt

# Download the content
appmctl files get --name mapping.txt -o ./mapping.txt

# Replace metadata, then clear it
appmctl files update --name mapping.txt --metadata '{"channel":"stable"}'
appmctl files update --name mapping.txt --metadata null

# Remove the file
appmctl files delete --name mapping.txt`} />

  <h3><code>appmctl assets</code></h3>
  <p>
    Assets are the app icon and splash screen. Each app has at most one of each, so
    <code>upload</code> replaces the existing asset.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl assets list [--app APP]
appmctl assets show [--app APP] --type icon|splashscreen [-o FILE]
appmctl assets download [--app APP] --type icon|splashscreen [--variant VARIANT] [-o FILE]
appmctl assets upload [--app APP] --type icon|splashscreen --file FILE
appmctl assets delete [--app APP] --type icon|splashscreen`} />
  <p>
    Besides the original file, the platform generates per-platform variants
    (<code>ios-icon-40</code>, <code>android-icon-mdpi</code>, and so on). <code>list</code> prints
    the download URL for the original plus every variant that has actually been generated, and
    <code>download --variant</code> fetches one of them (default: <code>original</code>).
    <code>show</code> streams the original inline for previewing.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`# Discover which variants exist
appmctl --json assets list --app customer-app

# Upload or replace an icon and a splash screen
appmctl assets upload --type icon --file ./icon.png
appmctl assets upload --type splashscreen --file ./splash.png

# Download the original and a generated variant
appmctl assets download --type icon -o icon.png
appmctl assets download --type icon --variant ios-icon-40 -o icon-40.png

# Delete an asset
appmctl assets delete --type splashscreen`} />

  <h3><code>appmctl apps</code></h3>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl apps list
appmctl apps get SLUG
appmctl apps create --name NAME [--slug SLUG] [--description DESC] [--metadata JSON]
appmctl apps update SLUG [--name NAME] [--description DESC] [--metadata JSON]
appmctl apps delete SLUG`} />
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`# List team apps
appmctl apps list

# Show app details
appmctl apps get customer-app

# Create an app
appmctl apps create --name "Customer App" --slug customer-app

# Update an app
appmctl apps update customer-app --name "Updated Name"

# Delete an app
appmctl apps delete customer-app`} />

  <h3><code>appmctl credentials</code></h3>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl credentials list [--app APP]
appmctl credentials get [--app APP] CREDENTIAL_ID
appmctl credentials create [--app APP] --type TYPE --profile PROFILE [--data-file FILE|-] [--file FILE]
appmctl credentials update [--app APP] CREDENTIAL_ID --data-file FILE|-
appmctl credentials delete [--app APP] CREDENTIAL_ID
appmctl credentials download [--app APP] CREDENTIAL_ID [-o FILE]
appmctl credentials upload [--app APP] CREDENTIAL_ID --file FILE
appmctl credentials types`} />
  <p>
    Pass credential data through <code>--data-file</code>, either from a file or from stdin with
    <code>-</code>. The inline <code>--data JSON</code> form still exists but is deprecated: process
    arguments are visible to other users and are often captured in CI logs.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`# List credentials and the available types
appmctl credentials list --app customer-app
appmctl credentials types

# Create a credential, reading secret data from stdin
printf '%s' '{"keystore_password":"secret"}' | \\
  appmctl credentials create --app customer-app \\
    --type android_keystore --profile production \\
    --data-file - --file ./release.jks

# Rotate the secret payload of an existing credential
appmctl credentials update cred-001 --data-file ./new-data.json

# Download and replace the attached file
appmctl credentials download cred-001 -o keystore.jks
appmctl credentials upload cred-001 --file ./keystore.jks

# Delete a credential
appmctl credentials delete cred-001`} />

  <h3><code>appmctl integrations</code></h3>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl integrations list
appmctl integrations get INTEGRATION_ID
appmctl integrations repos --integration-id ID
appmctl integrations workflows --integration-id ID
appmctl integrations refs --integration-id ID`} />
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`# List integrations
appmctl integrations list

# Inspect one integration
appmctl integrations get int-001

# Discover repositories, workflows, and branches/tags
appmctl integrations repos --integration-id int-001
appmctl integrations workflows --integration-id int-001
appmctl integrations refs --integration-id int-001`} />

  <h3><code>appmctl pipelines</code></h3>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl pipelines list --integration-id ID
appmctl pipelines get --integration-id ID PIPELINE_ID
appmctl pipelines create --integration-id ID --name NAME [--config JSON]
appmctl pipelines update --integration-id ID PIPELINE_ID [--name NAME] [--config JSON]
appmctl pipelines delete --integration-id ID PIPELINE_ID
appmctl pipelines run --integration-id ID PIPELINE_ID [--inputs JSON]
appmctl pipelines inputs --integration-id ID PIPELINE_ID`} />
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`# List and inspect pipelines
appmctl pipelines list --integration-id int-001
appmctl pipelines get --integration-id int-001 pipe-001

# Create and update a pipeline
appmctl pipelines create --integration-id int-001 \\
  --name "Android release" \\
  --config '{"workflow":"release.yml","ref":"main"}'
appmctl pipelines update --integration-id int-001 pipe-001 --name "Android nightly"

# Check which inputs a run requires, then trigger it
appmctl pipelines inputs --integration-id int-001 pipe-001
appmctl pipelines run --integration-id int-001 pipe-001 \\
  --inputs '{"profile":"production"}'

# Delete a pipeline
appmctl pipelines delete --integration-id int-001 pipe-001`} />

  <h3><code>appmctl webhooks</code></h3>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl webhooks list
appmctl webhooks get WEBHOOK_ID
appmctl webhooks create --url URL [--events JSON] [--app SLUG]
appmctl webhooks update WEBHOOK_ID [--url URL] [--events JSON]
appmctl webhooks delete WEBHOOK_ID
appmctl webhooks test WEBHOOK_ID
appmctl webhooks rotate WEBHOOK_ID
appmctl webhooks deliveries WEBHOOK_ID
appmctl webhooks redeliver --webhook-id ID DELIVERY_ID`} />
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`# Create an endpoint, optionally scoped to one app
appmctl webhooks create --url https://example.com/hook \\
  --events '["build.completed","build.failed"]' \\
  --app customer-app

# Change the URL or the subscribed events
appmctl webhooks update wh-001 --events '["build.completed"]'

# Send a test delivery and rotate the HMAC signing secret
appmctl webhooks test wh-001
appmctl webhooks rotate wh-001

# Inspect deliveries and retry one
appmctl webhooks deliveries wh-001
appmctl webhooks redeliver --webhook-id wh-001 del-001`} />
  <p>
    See the <a href="/guides/webhooks">Webhooks guide</a> for the payload format and
    signature verification.
  </p>

  <h3><code>appmctl request</code> (legacy)</h3>
  <p>Generic access to any team endpoint. Useful for endpoints not yet covered by dedicated subcommands.</p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`# List apps with pretty JSON
appmctl --json request GET apps

# Create an app with inline JSON
appmctl request POST apps \\
  --data '{"name":"Customer App"}' --json

# Read a request body from a file or stdin
appmctl request POST apps --data-file ./app.json
printf '%s' '{"name":"Customer App"}' | appmctl request POST apps --data-file -

# Upload a multipart file
appmctl request POST apps/customer-app/assets/icon \\
  --file file=icon.png --json

# Download a binary response
appmctl request GET apps/customer-app/assets/icon --output icon.png`} />
  <p>
    Only one body kind can be selected, but multipart text and file fields can be mixed and
    repeated. Prefer <code>--data-file</code> over <code>--data</code> for anything sensitive.
  </p>

  <h3><code>appmctl stream</code> (legacy)</h3>
  <p>
    Runs a command without <code>eval</code>, mirrors its combined stdout/stderr locally, and
    uploads the output to a manual build log while the command is running.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl stream --app APP --build-id ID [options] -- COMMAND [ARG ...]`} />
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Option</th><th>Description</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>--build-id ID</code></td><td>Existing manual build UUID (required)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--step NAME</code></td><td>Add visible start/finish separators to the log</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--chunk-bytes BYTES</code></td><td>Upload chunk size, 1..262144 (default: <code>65536</code>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--manage-status</code></td><td>Set <code>running</code> before the command and <code>completed</code>/<code>failed</code> after</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--track</code></td><td>Poll build status and abort if the build is cancelled</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--command</code></td><td>Treat all remaining arguments as the command</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`# Stream a build step
appmctl stream \\
  --app customer-app \\
  --build-id "$APP_MANAGER_BUILD_ID" \\
  --step 'JavaScript build' \\
  -- pnpm build

# Multiple steps on the same build
appmctl stream --build-id BUILD_ID --step Install -- pnpm install
appmctl stream --build-id BUILD_ID --step Test -- pnpm test
appmctl stream --build-id BUILD_ID --step Build -- pnpm build`} />
  <p>
    The CLI exits with the command's status. Use <code>sh -c</code> explicitly for pipes,
    redirects, variable expansion, or other shell syntax.
  </p>

  <h2>CI/CD examples</h2>

  <h3>GitHub Actions</h3>
  <CodeBlock lang="yaml" code={`- name: Install App Manager CLI
  shell: bash
  run: |
    curl -fsSL \\
      https://raw.githubusercontent.com/karerckor/app-manager/main/libs/cli/install.sh | \\
      APPMCTL_INSTALL_DIR="$RUNNER_TEMP/appmctl-bin" bash
    echo "$RUNNER_TEMP/appmctl-bin" >> "$GITHUB_PATH"

- name: Build and stream logs
  env:
    APP_MANAGER_API_KEY: $&#123;&#123; secrets.APP_MANAGER_API_KEY &#125;&#125;
    APP_MANAGER_TEAM: mobile-team
    APP_MANAGER_APP: customer-app
    APP_MANAGER_INSTANCE_URL: $&#123;&#123; vars.APP_MANAGER_INSTANCE_URL &#125;&#125;
    APP_MANAGER_BUILD_ID: $&#123;&#123; inputs.app_manager_build_id &#125;&#125;
  run: |
    appmctl stream \\
      --build-id "$APP_MANAGER_BUILD_ID" \\
      --step Build \\
      -- pnpm build`} />

  <h3>GitLab CI</h3>
  <CodeBlock lang="yaml" code={`before_script:
  - curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/karerckor/app-manager/main/libs/cli/install.sh | APPMCTL_INSTALL_DIR="$CI_PROJECT_DIR/.appmctl-bin" bash
  - export PATH="$CI_PROJECT_DIR/.appmctl-bin:$PATH"

build:
  script:
    - appmctl stream --build-id "$APP_MANAGER_BUILD_ID" --step Build -- pnpm build`} />

  <h3>Full manual build lifecycle</h3>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`# 1. Create the build
BUILD_ID=$(
  appmctl --json builds create \\
    --app "$APP_MANAGER_APP" \\
    --profile production \\
    --context '{"runner":"ci"}' \\
    --tag ci |
    jq -er '.data.id'
)
export BUILD_ID

# 2. Stream build steps
appmctl stream --build-id "$BUILD_ID" --manage-status --step Install -- pnpm install
appmctl stream --build-id "$BUILD_ID" --step Build -- pnpm build

# 3. Upload the artifact
appmctl artifacts upload \\
  --build-id "$BUILD_ID" \\
  --name app-release.aab \\
  --file build/app-release.aab

# 4. Attach the mapping file to the app's media library
appmctl files upload \\
  --file build/mapping.txt \\
  --metadata '{"build_id":"'"$BUILD_ID"'"}'`} />

  <h3>Gating a job on another build</h3>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`# Block until the upstream build finishes; a non-zero exit fails the job
appmctl builds wait "$UPSTREAM_BUILD_ID" --timeout 3600

# Or skip expensive work when the build is no longer active
appmctl builds when-running "$BUILD_ID" -- ./scripts/upload-to-store.sh`} />

  <h2>Exit status and errors</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Successful operations exit <code>0</code>.</li>
    <li>
      Commands exit non-zero for local validation errors, <code>curl</code> failures, non-2xx HTTP
      responses, invalid JSON requested through <code>--json</code>, or output errors.
    </li>
    <li><code>stream</code> returns the wrapped command's status whenever the command fails. If the command succeeds but log upload, capture, or a managed status update fails, it exits <code>1</code>.</li>
    <li><code>builds wait</code> and <code>builds when-running</code> use the dedicated exit codes listed above.</li>
    <li><code>--dry-run</code> validates configuration and arguments but neither sends a request nor executes a wrapped command.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    For unsuccessful HTTP responses the body is printed to stderr when available, followed by the
    status. Typical meanings:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Status</th><th>Common cause</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>401</code></td><td>Invalid or revoked API key</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>403</code></td><td>Missing API scope or team permission</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>404</code></td><td>Wrong team/app/build path, or a cross-team resource</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>409</code></td><td>Manual writes attempted on an automatic build, or a conflicting terminal state</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>422</code></td><td>API validation failure</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The client does not retry normal requests. Use an outer CI retry only when the operation is safe
    or explicitly idempotent — build creation is idempotent when the same <code>--request-uuid</code>
    is reused.
  </p>

  <h2>Current boundaries</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>stream</code> works only with builds in <code>manual</code> reporting mode. Automatic provider builds reject manual status and log writes.</li>
    <li>Output is combined stdout/stderr; separate streams and terminal control sequences are not preserved.</li>
    <li>The API accepts at most 256 KiB per log chunk and 5 MB per complete build log. The CLI does not truncate automatically when the server limit is reached.</li>
    <li>A response is treated as bytes unless <code>--json</code> is selected; content type does not change output behavior.</li>
    <li>There is no config file or credential store. Configuration is deliberately explicit through environment variables and flags.</li>
    <li><code>request</code> remains available for endpoints not yet covered by dedicated subcommands.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Security</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The API key is accepted only through <code>APP_MANAGER_API_KEY</code>, never as a CLI argument</li>
    <li>Authorization headers and inline request bodies are written to mode-<code>0600</code> files in a mode-<code>0700</code> temporary directory before being passed to <code>curl</code></li>
    <li>Dry-run output redacts all sensitive headers</li>
    <li>Temporary state is removed on normal exit; treat build runners as sensitive because wrapped commands and downloaded responses may contain secrets</li>
    <li>Use HTTPS outside local development and pin installer revisions in production CI</li>
  </ul>
